#!/bin/sh
# Scaffold a course as *data*: one file, many outputs.
#
# A course owns NO mathematics of its own, and it is not a directory: it
# is one file, notes/courses/<slug>.org, whose `* Programme' section
# lists -- in teaching order -- id: links to notes that already exist in
# notes/math/. The four documents are composed from that list at export
# time (bin/cerebro-course-export), so nothing is ever copied and no
# generated file is committed.
#
# What the file is for, in one line each:
#   metadata      #+COURSE_TITLE / #+MSC / #+KEYWORDS, written once
#   prose         what the subject is, and what one can do having read it
#   * Programme   the manifest: the notes, in order, as id: links
#   * Appendices  optional: notes typeset after \appendix, lettered
#
# Deliberately absent: instructor, credits, term, level, prerequisites,
# textbook, examination format, marking, deadlines. THESE ARE NOTES.
# A course here is an ordering of mathematics, not a module description:
# none of that administrative apparatus is about the subject, all of it
# goes out of date before the mathematics does, and it belongs wherever
# your institution keeps such things.
#
# Usage: bin/cerebro-course SLUG ["Course Title"]
#   bin/cerebro-course analysis-B "Mathematical Analysis B"
set -eu

repo_root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)
slug=${1:?"usage: cerebro-course SLUG [\"Course Title\"]"}
# A path is accepted too, so `cerebro-course notes/courses/x' keeps working.
slug=$(basename "$slug" .org)
title=${2:-$slug}
dir="$repo_root/notes/courses"
course="$dir/$slug.org"
today=$(date +%Y-%m-%d)

mkdir -p "$dir"

if [ -e "$course" ]; then
    echo "cerebro-course: ${course#"$repo_root"/} already exists -- leaving it untouched"
else
    cat > "$course" <<EOF
#+TITLE: ${title} -- syllabus
#+COURSE_TITLE: ${title}
#+MSC:
#+KEYWORDS:
#+DATE: ${today}

* Description
#+begin_comment
One paragraph: what the subject is and what holds it together. (Comment
blocks are excluded from every export -- replace with real prose.)
#+end_comment

* Objectives
By the end of the course, a student can:
-

* Programme
#+begin_comment
The course, as an ordered index into notes/math/ -- this section IS the
manifest the lecture notes, the slides and the handbook are composed
from, at export time. There is no generated second file to keep in step.

Write the atomic notes first (C-c m t / C-c m d), then list them here in
teaching order, linked by id: (C-c n i inserts one). Sub-headings become
the chapters; every link becomes an #+INCLUDE of that note, so nothing
is ever copied and editing the note updates every course that teaches it.

  ** Continuity
  - [[id:...][Limits and continuity]]
  ** Inner product spaces
  - [[id:...][Inner product space]]
  - [[id:...][Cauchy--Schwarz inequality]]
#+end_comment

* Appendices
#+begin_comment
Optional, and written exactly like the programme: background the course
uses without proving. Composed after \appendix, so these chapters are
lettered rather than numbered. Delete this section if there are none.
#+end_comment

* Bibliography
#+begin_comment
Cite with [cite:@key] against the central bib/references.bib (C-c b i
inserts a citation, C-c b n adds a new entry). Every non-slide profile
prints the reference list.
#+end_comment
EOF
    echo "wrote ${course#"$repo_root"/}"
fi

cat <<EOF

Course scaffolded. Next: write the notes, then list them.

  1. atomic notes in notes/math/    C-c m t (theorem), C-c m d (definition)
  2. list them under '* Programme'  in ${course#"$repo_root"/}   (C-c n i inserts an id: link)

Then all four documents, from that one file:
  bin/cerebro-course-export ${course#"$repo_root"/}

or one at a time:
  make export FILE=${course#"$repo_root"/} PROFILE=syllabus
  make export FILE=${course#"$repo_root"/} PROFILE=lecture
  make export FILE=${course#"$repo_root"/} PROFILE=slides
  make export FILE=${course#"$repo_root"/} PROFILE=handbook
EOF
